  "slug": "mirna-dysregulation-e0fb",
  "summary": "Recent preprints resolve early synaptic, glial, and metabolic changes in Huntington, tau, TDP-43, and Alzheimer models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is early, cell-type-specific dysfunction in neurons and glia, resolved mainly through proteomic, transcriptomic and metabolic readouts. In R6/1 Huntington's mice, excitatory neurons reduce synaptic proteins before motor onset, while glia show decline in neuronal support proteins. Transgenic minipig putamen shows age-dependent transcriptome and proteome changes at 4 and 8 years. Metabolic studies add astrocytic involvement: deuterium MRS in AAV-induced astrogliosis and APP knock-in mice shows increased astrocytic acetate use, and another study links astrocytes to mitochondrial metabolic alterations in Alzheimer's disease. In tau models, early locus coeruleus tauopathy causes hyperexcitability through reduced potassium currents, and Kv7 activation lowers tau burden. The TDP-43 study extends this framing to lipid overload, linking dysfunction to maladaptive metabolic and cognitive responses to high-fat diet in bvFTD patients and knock-in mice.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
